<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John VI of Portugal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1767–1826</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as João VI</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John VI was regent of Portugal from 1799 and king from 1816 to 1826. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> invaded in 1807 he moved the court to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, opened its ports and in 1815 raised it to a kingdom within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-kingdom-of-portugal-brazil-and-the-algarves">United Kingdom of Portugal, Brazil and the Algarves</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberal-revolution-of-1820">Liberal Revolution</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto">Porto</a> forced his return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> in 1821, and his son Pedro declared Brazil's independence in 1822.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-vi-of-portugal.jpg" alt="John VI of Portugal" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Albert Gregorius · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Retrato_de_D._Joao_VI_-_Gregorius,_Albertus_Jacob_Frans.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
